      "source_excerpt": "protocolSection.designModule.designInfo.interventionModelDescription and outcomesModule: \"Following a screening period of up to 30 days, eligible participants will be randomly assigned to one of two groups for up to 8 weeks.\" \"During this time-called the Double-Blind Period-2 out of 3 participants will receive the active treatment (tapinarof cream, 1%), while 1 out of 3 will receive a vehicle cream (placebo).\" \"Following completion of the Double-blind period, participants will enter the 48-week Open Label Period and will be treated as needed.\" \"Baseline to last planned visit in the Double-Blind Period, up to 8 weeks.\" \"Percentage of participants who enter with or achieve have a validated Investigator Global Assessment for Atopic Dermatitis (vIGA-AD) score of clear or almost clear (0 or 1) and a minimum 2-grade Improvement at least once during the Open-Label Period\" \"Baseline to last planned visit in the Double-Blind Period, up to 8 weeks.\" \"Baseline to last planned visit in the Double-Blind Period, up to 8 weeks.\" \"Baseline to last planned visit in the Double-Blind Period, up to 8 weeks.\" -- Screening is up to 30 days. Double-blind treatment lasts up to 8 weeks, followed by a 48-week open-label period; the open-label period is recorded as continuation treatment because the schema has no open-label period name. The registered primary outcomes have different horizons: one is through the last planned double-blind visit (up to 8 weeks), while the other is assessed at least once during the open-label period (up to 56 weeks); no single primary endpoint week is assigned. Week 8 is stated for secondary outcomes. The record gives no visit schedule. Total duration and absolute end weeks are left unset because the 48-week open-label clock begins after a double-blind period of up to 8 weeks, following screening. No full visit table appears in the supplied CT.gov record.",
